--- `concat[a, b]` with `a` a list of 64-bit ints and `b` a list of growing ints
--- (an item past 64 bits, or a list the width pass holds as growing): the compiled
--- program does not build — the first list is not widened to the second's items.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     [0, 1, 1180591620717411303424]
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types
---       (rustc: `crow_runtime::core::concat(range_to(2), &xs[..])`: expected `&[i64]`,
---       found `&[crow_runtime::int::Int]`)
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the interpreter's line in both modes.
---
--- The growing list first, `concat[xs, range[2]]`, builds and runs. Met in a program
--- with no number past 64 bits: `concat[range[n], filled[n, -1]]`, where the one copy
--- of a helper `filled[n, value]` is also handed a value the width pass holds as
--- growing (a maximum of a parameter's weights), so all its answers are growing ints.

xs = []
xs = push[xs, 2 ^ 70]
log[concat[range[2], xs]]
